==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈbɪkə/|lang=en}}
*: {{rhymes|ɪkə(r)|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|bikeren}} ‘to attack’, from {{etyl|dum|en}} {{m|nl|bicken}} ‘to stab, attack’ (modern {{m|nl|bikken}} ‘to hack’), from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*bikjaną}} (compare Old English {{m|ang|becca}} ‘pickax’, German {{m|de|picken}} ‘to peck, pick at’, Old Norse {{m|non|bikkja}} ‘to plunge into water’), from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*bʰeg-}} ‘to smash, break’.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[quarrel]] in a [[tiresome]], insulting manner.
#: ''They '''bickered''' about dinner every evening.''
#* Barrow
#*: petty things about which men cark and '''bicker'''
# To move [[tremulously]], quiver, shimmer (of a water stream, of a flame)
#* XIX cent, ''[[s:The Brook (Tennyson)|The Brook]]'', by [[w:Alfred, Lord Tennyson|Tennyson]]
#*: I come from haunts of coot and hern, / I make a sudden sally, / And sparkle out among the fern, / To '''bicker''' down a valley.
#* Thomson
#*: They [streamlets] '''bickered''' through the sunny shade.
# To skirmish; to [[exchange blows]]; to fight.
#* Holland
#*: Two eagles had a conflict, and '''bickered''' together.

